1
00:00:21,951 --> 00:00:25,541
I see. So, Canute is dead?

2
00:00:28,091 --> 00:00:32,091
No. He went missing, to be precise.

3
00:00:33,051 --> 00:00:36,431
Ragnar's unit tried to rescue Canute,

4
00:00:36,511 --> 00:00:39,311
and engaged Thorkell in battle at Marlborough.

5
00:00:40,351 --> 00:00:41,971
In the midst of the confusion,

6
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:44,691
His Highness the Prince went missing.

7
00:00:48,111 --> 00:00:52,571
Then aren't they still surrounded by Thorkell's men?

8
00:00:52,651 --> 00:00:56,781
Yes. However, we didn't see anyone among
Thorkell's men

9
00:00:56,861 --> 00:01:01,491
who looked like he could be the prince for two days.

10
00:01:03,371 --> 00:01:07,001
He may still be alive and is waiting for help.

11
00:01:08,081 --> 00:01:10,591
However, even if we were to send help,

12
00:01:10,671 --> 00:01:13,591
we'd have to wait until spring.

13
00:01:13,671 --> 00:01:16,131
I'm sure you must be very worried,

14
00:01:16,221 --> 00:01:18,511
Your Highness.

15
00:01:22,681 --> 00:01:24,851
If Canute

16
00:01:24,931 --> 00:01:27,481
had been as talented

17
00:01:27,561 --> 00:01:29,771
as his brother Harald,

18
00:01:31,151 --> 00:01:36,241
he might not have
ended up missing.

19
00:03:13,751 --> 00:03:15,171
Wow! It's snowing!

20
00:03:15,251 --> 00:03:16,251
AFTER YULE

21
00:03:16,341 --> 00:03:19,511
The war is ending!

22
00:03:19,591 --> 00:03:20,721
Winter.

23
00:03:20,801 --> 00:03:22,591
DECEMBER 1013
GLOUCESTER, EARLDOM OF MERCIA

24
00:03:22,681 --> 00:03:26,141
You're cold enough to freeze even a warrior's heart.

25
00:03:26,221 --> 00:03:28,891
Oh, winter!

26
00:03:28,971 --> 00:03:32,521
Captain. Roasted pork or boiled pork.
What do you want?

27
00:03:32,601 --> 00:03:33,601
Give me both!

28
00:03:39,821 --> 00:03:42,571
I can't understand you. Swallow your food first.

29
00:03:42,661 --> 00:03:43,661
Okay.

30
00:03:45,821 --> 00:03:48,491
In any case,

31
00:03:48,581 --> 00:03:51,911
the fact that the prince escaped was a big loss to us.

32
00:03:52,001 --> 00:03:54,751
He didn't escape! You handed him over.

33
00:03:54,831 --> 00:03:59,921
That prince was a coward,
but he could have been useful.

34
00:04:00,011 --> 00:04:03,471
We could have put him on a pedestal
and started a new dynasty.

35
00:04:03,551 --> 00:04:06,391
It would have helped gather soldiers for an army
at least.

36
00:04:06,471 --> 00:04:09,181
If you're the one who's going to carry him,

37
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:11,431
you'd be pretty powerful.

38
00:04:11,521 --> 00:04:13,601
Indeed. What?

39
00:04:13,691 --> 00:04:16,271
Hey, you! English girl!

40
00:04:16,361 --> 00:04:17,651
Yes?

41
00:04:17,731 --> 00:04:20,111
Beer! Bring a keg!

42
00:04:20,191 --> 00:04:23,201
Excuse me! They ordered another keg!

43
00:04:23,281 --> 00:04:25,741
Another one? Damn!

44
00:04:25,821 --> 00:04:29,951
Those rascals! They're going to eat through
the entire city!

45
00:04:30,041 --> 00:04:33,001
This is robbery!

46
00:04:33,081 --> 00:04:34,671
Stop crying!

47
00:04:38,501 --> 00:04:43,421
If I was going to lose the prince anyway,
I should have given him to the English instead.

48
00:04:45,431 --> 00:04:46,891
What?

49
00:04:46,971 --> 00:04:49,511
You're so difficult.

50
00:04:49,601 --> 00:04:53,481
As soon as the fire starts to die down,
you go looking for another spark.

51
00:04:53,561 --> 00:04:56,061
Of course, you bastard!

52
00:04:56,151 --> 00:04:58,861
We're warriors!

53
00:04:58,941 --> 00:05:01,611
A warrior is a warrior when he's on the battlefield!

54
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:05,781
But you know...

55
00:05:05,901 --> 00:05:08,741
I heard some bad news about that.

56
00:05:10,241 --> 00:05:13,041
They're going to recognize King Sweyn
as the Bretwalda

57
00:05:13,121 --> 00:05:16,871
at the next Witenagemot in Wessex.

58
00:05:16,961 --> 00:05:18,251
What?

59
00:05:18,331 --> 00:05:20,671
That's total surrender!

60
00:05:20,751 --> 00:05:24,381
That's right. Denmark is going
to swallow England whole.

61
00:05:24,471 --> 00:05:28,221
The nobility want to end the war, even if it means
surrendering the throne.

62
00:05:30,471 --> 00:05:33,971
Every time I get a bad feeling about something,
why does it always come true?

63
00:05:34,061 --> 00:05:38,191
What are you going to do? Attack King Sweyn,
even if it means certain death?

64
00:05:38,271 --> 00:05:39,521
You idiot!

65
00:05:39,611 --> 00:05:43,571
I love fighting, but I hate losing!

66
00:05:43,651 --> 00:05:45,441
When I fight, I fight to win!

67
00:05:51,371 --> 00:05:53,241
This is no fun.

68
00:05:53,331 --> 00:05:57,711
I wish the world would be at war forever.

69
00:05:59,041 --> 00:06:01,541
It's a messenger! Is the lord here?

70
00:06:07,671 --> 00:06:09,591
Anything out of the ordinary, Ear?

71
00:06:09,681 --> 00:06:11,801
There sure is. It's damn cold.

72
00:06:13,101 --> 00:06:14,311
The snow absorbs sounds.

73
00:06:14,391 --> 00:06:17,351
NORTH PART OF EARLDOM OF MERCIA
ASKELADD'S ARMY CONTROLLED FARM VILLAGE

74
00:06:17,431 --> 00:06:18,941
Even I can't hear that far anymore.

75
00:06:19,021 --> 00:06:21,941
It's better than keeping guards all over the place.

76
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:24,191
We can avoid giving away our position.

77
00:06:25,361 --> 00:06:28,991
Then do something about those guys.

78
00:06:29,071 --> 00:06:31,871
They're obviously not farmers.

79
00:06:31,951 --> 00:06:36,581
-Monks? Keep an eye on them, too.
-Father, forgive us.

80
00:06:36,661 --> 00:06:40,211
"Forgive us." Hear that?

81
00:06:40,291 --> 00:06:44,671
Must they constantly beg for forgiveness?

82
00:06:44,751 --> 00:06:46,001
What a pain.

83
00:06:48,761 --> 00:06:51,091
Oh, God Most High,

84
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:53,851
Our Father in Heaven,

85
00:06:53,931 --> 00:06:56,891
please gather the souls of the villagers

86
00:06:56,971 --> 00:06:59,941
who lie in rest here to your side.

87
00:07:00,021 --> 00:07:04,441
Please save the souls of the men who killed
these people.

88
00:07:04,521 --> 00:07:07,481
Please forgive us for our sins.

89
00:07:10,951 --> 00:07:14,321
This isn't the time to be praying for someone else,
you bastards.

90
00:07:21,211 --> 00:07:22,791
Father.

91
00:07:22,881 --> 00:07:26,341
Can you hear my voice?

92
00:07:29,091 --> 00:07:30,631
Our Father.

93
00:07:31,431 --> 00:07:32,841
You

94
00:07:32,931 --> 00:07:36,261
created us in your image.

95
00:07:37,351 --> 00:07:40,351
But you didn't give us your power.

96
00:07:42,271 --> 00:07:43,311
Father.

97
00:07:44,521 --> 00:07:48,361
If you love both good men and bad men equally,

98
00:07:49,481 --> 00:07:53,281
why do you test us, despite our powerlessness?

99
00:07:54,621 --> 00:07:55,621
Father.

100
00:07:56,871 --> 00:07:58,701
Can you

101
00:07:58,791 --> 00:08:01,211
hear my voice?

102
00:08:02,501 --> 00:08:03,791
Our Father.

103
00:08:05,251 --> 00:08:07,041
I doubt

104
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:10,301
-your love.
-Enough!

105
00:08:20,471 --> 00:08:22,351
Did you say...

106
00:08:22,431 --> 00:08:23,851
you doubt the Father?

107
00:08:28,941 --> 00:08:33,201
And you call yourself a Christian?
You should fear the Lord!

108
00:08:33,281 --> 00:08:36,201
We must not doubt the Father's love!

109
00:08:40,161 --> 00:08:41,161
Every father...

110
00:08:42,541 --> 00:08:46,581
Every father loves his own child.

111
00:08:50,841 --> 00:08:51,841
Your Highness...

112
00:08:54,881 --> 00:08:56,511
Oh! Wait!

113
00:09:07,151 --> 00:09:08,311
Father...

114
00:09:11,231 --> 00:09:12,821
An army of Danes?

115
00:09:12,901 --> 00:09:14,321
Really?

116
00:09:14,401 --> 00:09:16,951
Yes. That's what the message said.

117
00:09:17,031 --> 00:09:21,451
They've occupied a village
at the head of the Severn River.

118
00:09:21,541 --> 00:09:24,911
We've received a request for reinforcements
from the local lord.

119
00:09:25,001 --> 00:09:28,041
According to one of the villagers who survived,

120
00:09:28,131 --> 00:09:30,921
the enemy has around 100 to 200 men.

121
00:09:31,001 --> 00:09:35,591
Soldiers from neighboring areas are already
heading there,

122
00:09:35,671 --> 00:09:39,431
but we wanted to ask you to come too, Thorkell,
because of your military fame.

123
00:09:39,511 --> 00:09:42,931
-What do you think?
-Nothing, really.

124
00:09:43,021 --> 00:09:47,021
There aren't a lot of Danish units who would
act alone there

125
00:09:47,101 --> 00:09:49,401
at this time of year.

126
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:52,861
They feigned to go by sea,
but they're heading to Gainsborough by land.

127
00:09:53,861 --> 00:09:57,611
They must have been snowed in and are stuck.

128
00:09:57,701 --> 00:09:59,571
I'm almost certain it's...

129
00:10:03,451 --> 00:10:06,501
Thorfinn and Canute.

130
00:10:06,581 --> 00:10:10,381
All right! Wake everyone up! Get ready to march!

131
00:10:11,381 --> 00:10:12,631
And...

132
00:10:12,711 --> 00:10:14,631
Hey, you.

133
00:10:14,711 --> 00:10:17,471
Pack us some lunch.

134
00:10:17,551 --> 00:10:20,641
For 500 men. Five days will be enough.

135
00:10:20,721 --> 00:10:23,141
Five hundred?

136
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:27,481
Here we go.

137
00:10:27,561 --> 00:10:31,771
This is fun. If we do this right,

138
00:10:32,941 --> 00:10:35,981
we can enjoy this war for a long time!

139
00:10:46,161 --> 00:10:48,331
How many men are there? Ear?

140
00:10:48,411 --> 00:10:51,331
Who knows? Maybe 50, or 100.

141
00:10:51,421 --> 00:10:55,301
They're extending their line to the east along the forest.

142
00:10:55,381 --> 00:10:58,131
They discovered us quickly.

143
00:10:58,221 --> 00:10:59,631
Did we make a mistake somewhere?

144
00:10:59,721 --> 00:11:01,801
I don't know...

145
00:11:01,891 --> 00:11:04,641
They found us. We can't change that.

146
00:11:04,721 --> 00:11:06,641
We're leaving?

147
00:11:06,721 --> 00:11:08,981
Tonight? That's impossible!

148
00:11:09,061 --> 00:11:11,481
I've only finished two sleds!

149
00:11:11,561 --> 00:11:13,561
I just started on the third one yesterday!

150
00:11:13,651 --> 00:11:17,731
Really? Thanks. Then finish them by tonight.

151
00:11:17,821 --> 00:11:19,901
We'll abandon the ones we can't finish.

152
00:11:19,991 --> 00:11:21,991
I said that's impossible!

153
00:11:22,071 --> 00:11:24,121
We only have two carpenters!

154
00:11:24,201 --> 00:11:26,991
The English army found us.

155
00:11:27,081 --> 00:11:29,161
-They found us?
-Yeah.

156
00:11:29,251 --> 00:11:31,921
They came through the snow.

157
00:11:32,001 --> 00:11:34,171
They must really hate the Danes.

158
00:11:34,251 --> 00:11:35,291
Impossible...

159
00:11:35,381 --> 00:11:37,881
We must hurry, or we'll miss our chance
to escape.

160
00:11:39,211 --> 00:11:40,841
Understand? Hurry and finish.

161
00:11:40,921 --> 00:11:42,721
Okay.

162
00:11:42,801 --> 00:11:45,801
Bjorn! Prepare the men for battle.

163
00:11:45,891 --> 00:11:49,771
Capture their commander.
I want to find out how they found us.

164
00:11:49,851 --> 00:11:51,561
Understood.

165
00:11:51,641 --> 00:11:53,651
Wait, Bjorn.

166
00:11:53,731 --> 00:11:55,771
One more thing.

167
00:11:55,861 --> 00:11:57,151
This is a good opportunity.

168
00:11:57,231 --> 00:11:59,571
Let's do what we discussed as well.

169
00:12:01,741 --> 00:12:02,781
They found us?

170
00:12:03,861 --> 00:12:05,911
This is bad.

171
00:12:05,991 --> 00:12:09,911
We'll be fine! The English are weak.

172
00:12:10,001 --> 00:12:12,371
No. That's not what I mean.

173
00:12:12,461 --> 00:12:14,671
I was talking about Askeladd's luck.

174
00:12:14,751 --> 00:12:16,251
Luck?

175
00:12:16,331 --> 00:12:18,671
First the heavy snowfall, and now this.

176
00:12:18,751 --> 00:12:22,801
This is the first time his plans have ever been ruined.

177
00:12:29,641 --> 00:12:32,981
Oh! What a tasty-looking rabbit!

178
00:12:33,061 --> 00:12:37,311
-Are you the one who caught it, Thorfinn?
-What?

179
00:12:37,401 --> 00:12:40,861
Are you going to eat it alone? Are you going to roast it?

180
00:12:40,941 --> 00:12:43,281
Why do you care?

181
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:45,241
It's not good to eat nothing but meat!

182
00:12:45,321 --> 00:12:47,991
Boil it with some vegetables!

183
00:12:48,071 --> 00:12:50,621
I'll make some soup with that rabbit.

184
00:12:51,911 --> 00:12:56,001
Shut up. I can eat my own food however I want.

185
00:12:56,081 --> 00:12:59,381
You're going to borrow someone's stove anyway, right?

186
00:12:59,461 --> 00:13:00,631
Come over here!

187
00:13:04,421 --> 00:13:07,511
Your Highness! I've brought broad beans,
cabbage, and rabbit!

188
00:13:07,591 --> 00:13:08,601
Rabbit?

189
00:13:08,681 --> 00:13:11,311
Yes. Thorfinn caught it.

190
00:13:11,391 --> 00:13:13,731
I thought it would be good to add to the soup.

191
00:13:20,151 --> 00:13:21,321
Thanks.

192
00:13:21,401 --> 00:13:23,151
Drain the blood and remove its organs.

193
00:13:34,541 --> 00:13:37,541
The ingredients are simple, but it tastes good.

194
00:13:37,621 --> 00:13:38,881
It's because of the rabbit.

195
00:13:38,961 --> 00:13:42,421
I've grown tired of eating salted-cured meat. Thank you.

196
00:13:45,801 --> 00:13:47,551
What's wrong? Don't hold back.

197
00:13:47,631 --> 00:13:49,051
It's your rabbit.

198
00:13:56,351 --> 00:13:59,941
It's good, right? Catch us a deer next time!

199
00:14:02,191 --> 00:14:05,191
I thought royals only did the eating.

200
00:14:05,281 --> 00:14:08,361
It's my hobby. I don't usually cook.

201
00:14:08,451 --> 00:14:10,911
No one knows about it except Ragnar.

202
00:14:10,991 --> 00:14:13,541
You can't tell anyone either, okay?

203
00:14:13,621 --> 00:14:15,451
There's nothing wrong with it.

204
00:14:17,081 --> 00:14:18,751
A long time ago,

205
00:14:18,831 --> 00:14:20,671
I cooked some cod.

206
00:14:21,751 --> 00:14:23,501
It turned out well,

207
00:14:23,591 --> 00:14:25,761
so I brought it to my father.

208
00:14:33,561 --> 00:14:36,521
But His Highness didn't eat it.

209
00:14:36,601 --> 00:14:38,941
He was very angry.

210
00:14:39,021 --> 00:14:42,811
"You're the child of a king! Don't act like a slave!"
That's what he said.

211
00:14:43,981 --> 00:14:47,241
I wasn't supposed to cook after that.

212
00:14:48,451 --> 00:14:51,071
A king's child doesn't need to know how to do
things like this.

213
00:14:55,791 --> 00:14:57,251
At that time,

214
00:14:57,331 --> 00:15:00,581
His Highness must have been very busy.

215
00:15:00,671 --> 00:15:04,541
When this war is over, let's cook some poultry
and bring it to him.

216
00:15:04,631 --> 00:15:06,761
I'm sure he'd be happy.

217
00:15:11,011 --> 00:15:12,801
What kind of poultry?

218
00:15:12,891 --> 00:15:15,101
I've never cooked poultry before.

219
00:15:15,181 --> 00:15:17,561
Chicken, or duck.

220
00:15:17,641 --> 00:15:21,311
Do you remember the duck you ate during Yule?

221
00:15:21,391 --> 00:15:23,481
Yes. That was delicious.

222
00:15:23,561 --> 00:15:26,481
I asked the cook for his secret.

223
00:15:26,571 --> 00:15:29,191
He made good use of the broth.

224
00:15:29,281 --> 00:15:31,451
The broth? I see.

225
00:15:33,411 --> 00:15:35,331
Ragnar!

226
00:15:35,411 --> 00:15:37,581
You idiot! I have a message!

227
00:15:39,371 --> 00:15:41,371
What is it? What's wrong?

228
00:15:41,461 --> 00:15:43,671
The enemy has found us.

229
00:15:43,751 --> 00:15:46,291
A battle has begun in the woods to the east.

230
00:15:46,381 --> 00:15:47,551
What?

231
00:15:48,511 --> 00:15:50,511
They don't have a lot of men,

232
00:15:50,591 --> 00:15:52,431
but please be careful.

233
00:15:55,351 --> 00:15:56,721
What's going on?

234
00:15:56,811 --> 00:15:59,101
We've only been in this village for ten days!

235
00:15:59,181 --> 00:16:02,191
-How come they found us?
-How am I supposed to know?

236
00:16:05,151 --> 00:16:07,191
What's going on?

237
00:16:07,271 --> 00:16:10,191
We even killed all the villagers...

238
00:16:12,861 --> 00:16:14,451
In any case,

239
00:16:14,531 --> 00:16:17,701
we're going to move once we defeat the English.
Get ready.

240
00:16:17,781 --> 00:16:19,161
That's why I first said

241
00:16:19,241 --> 00:16:21,621
we should turn back to Wales!

242
00:16:21,711 --> 00:16:23,711
No. We're heading for Derby.

243
00:16:25,381 --> 00:16:27,091
He still wants to keep walking through

244
00:16:27,171 --> 00:16:29,341
enemy lands in this situation?

245
00:16:29,421 --> 00:16:32,671
Bring Askeladd here. Now!

246
00:16:32,761 --> 00:16:35,681
He's leading at the frontline right now.

247
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:38,971
If you want to talk to him, you should go
see him yourself.

248
00:16:39,061 --> 00:16:40,221
I'll show you the way.

249
00:16:43,441 --> 00:16:46,861
Unbelievable! You men are just like your leader!

250
00:16:46,941 --> 00:16:48,611
You don't have any manners!

251
00:16:48,691 --> 00:16:51,781
He can't even read his own luck!

252
00:16:51,861 --> 00:16:53,491
And he's a Norse warrior?

253
00:16:53,571 --> 00:16:56,871
I swear on my sword, I'm going to show him

254
00:16:56,951 --> 00:16:58,491
how foolish he is today!

255
00:17:02,451 --> 00:17:03,581
Hey!

256
00:17:03,661 --> 00:17:05,121
Hey!

257
00:17:05,211 --> 00:17:07,381
The sound of the battle is coming from that way!

258
00:17:10,801 --> 00:17:13,971
You finally noticed? You're so slow.

259
00:17:14,051 --> 00:17:16,591
What? You bastards! What are you--?

260
00:17:37,951 --> 00:17:39,951
You bastards...

261
00:17:40,031 --> 00:17:42,701
Sorry, Conehead.

262
00:17:42,791 --> 00:17:44,831
It's orders from Askeladd.

263
00:17:55,921 --> 00:17:59,221
Want to fight? We're okay with that.

264
00:17:59,301 --> 00:18:01,431
But you're going to die anyway.

265
00:18:08,061 --> 00:18:12,441
Bring Askeladd here! I have something important
to tell him.

266
00:18:12,521 --> 00:18:15,861
Is it your dying wish? I'll listen. Tell me.

267
00:18:15,941 --> 00:18:18,151
Just bring him here!

268
00:18:18,241 --> 00:18:20,781
It's related to His Highness the Prince.

269
00:18:20,871 --> 00:18:22,241
I need to talk to him directly.

270
00:18:24,951 --> 00:18:26,081
Hurry!

271
00:18:27,121 --> 00:18:29,871
Let's hear your dying wish.

272
00:18:39,181 --> 00:18:41,261
Shall we speak in private?

273
00:18:44,261 --> 00:18:46,221
The cries stopped.

274
00:18:46,311 --> 00:18:47,851
The battle is over.

275
00:18:53,861 --> 00:18:54,861
Unbelievable.

276
00:18:56,031 --> 00:18:58,441
The soup got cold.

277
00:19:01,111 --> 00:19:04,281
I'm grateful to you.

278
00:19:04,371 --> 00:19:08,751
You did a good job keeping that coward of a prince alive.

279
00:19:08,831 --> 00:19:10,831
Your death will cause him to grow

280
00:19:10,921 --> 00:19:13,251
even further.

281
00:19:14,631 --> 00:19:18,551
I will take over that job starting today,

282
00:19:18,631 --> 00:19:20,631
in place of you, who was

283
00:19:21,801 --> 00:19:23,681
"killed by the English."

284
00:19:26,681 --> 00:19:29,141
Swear to your god!

285
00:19:29,231 --> 00:19:32,441
Swear that you'll take over for me,

286
00:19:32,521 --> 00:19:33,901
and that...

287
00:19:35,731 --> 00:19:38,901
you'll protect His Highness Canute
at whatever the cost!

288
00:19:42,531 --> 00:19:46,031
I will protect His Highness Canute
at whatever the cost,

289
00:19:46,121 --> 00:19:49,661
and ensure that Ragnar the warrior
did not die in vain.

290
00:19:50,871 --> 00:19:51,961
Are you satisfied?

291
00:19:53,171 --> 00:19:55,211
It fills me with regret

292
00:19:55,291 --> 00:19:58,671
to leave His Highness with you.

293
00:19:58,761 --> 00:20:00,671
You should hurry.

294
00:20:00,761 --> 00:20:04,051
You've lost a lot of blood.

295
00:20:09,811 --> 00:20:11,601
Before you reach

296
00:20:12,941 --> 00:20:14,271
Gainsborough,

297
00:20:15,481 --> 00:20:18,481
you should prepare yourself...

298
00:20:21,401 --> 00:20:23,991
The court in Jelling

299
00:20:24,071 --> 00:20:27,081
are fighting over who will be the next king of Denmark

300
00:20:27,161 --> 00:20:29,991
and has split into two factions.

301
00:20:31,161 --> 00:20:33,831
One of the factions picked His Highness Canute

302
00:20:33,921 --> 00:20:35,831
as their leader.

303
00:20:36,881 --> 00:20:40,091
The other faction chose Canute's brother,

304
00:20:40,171 --> 00:20:41,711
Harald.

305
00:20:42,801 --> 00:20:44,931
They don't care about what

306
00:20:45,011 --> 00:20:47,681
either of them wants.

307
00:20:47,761 --> 00:20:49,261
It's just a power struggle

308
00:20:49,351 --> 00:20:51,601
amongst those traitors.

309
00:20:53,021 --> 00:20:55,021
His Highness the King feared

310
00:20:55,101 --> 00:20:58,611
that the Kingdom of Denmark would be split.

311
00:21:00,021 --> 00:21:04,241
His heart was probably already decided.

312
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:11,741
He decided to kill

313
00:21:11,831 --> 00:21:14,121
one of the princes.

314
00:21:14,211 --> 00:21:16,671
King Sweyn decided to kill the prince?

315
00:21:17,711 --> 00:21:19,671
The reason that the king

316
00:21:19,751 --> 00:21:22,551
took the prince to the battlefield with him

317
00:21:22,631 --> 00:21:25,471
was to have the prince die in battle.

318
00:21:26,681 --> 00:21:28,641
Be careful.

319
00:21:28,721 --> 00:21:31,641
Prince Canute's real enemy

320
00:21:32,561 --> 00:21:33,641
is now

321
00:21:34,561 --> 00:21:37,101
his father, King Sweyn

322
00:21:37,191 --> 00:21:39,061
himself.

323
00:21:40,821 --> 00:21:43,071
Go into exile.

324
00:21:43,151 --> 00:21:45,151
That's the only way now.

325
00:21:47,821 --> 00:21:49,911
In Gainsborough,

326
00:21:49,991 --> 00:21:53,371
my brother will make preparations

327
00:21:54,541 --> 00:21:56,541
for you...

328
00:22:05,091 --> 00:22:07,761
What's going on?

329
00:22:07,841 --> 00:22:10,761
You should have told me earlier.

330
00:22:13,181 --> 00:22:15,101
Please.

331
00:22:15,181 --> 00:22:16,431
Just one...

332
00:22:18,021 --> 00:22:19,231
last look...

333
00:22:20,231 --> 00:22:21,691
Let me see His Highness.

334
00:22:23,521 --> 00:22:25,191
To say goodbye...

335
00:22:26,901 --> 00:22:27,951
No.

336
00:22:33,371 --> 00:22:34,541
Your Highness...

337
00:22:35,541 --> 00:22:37,371
Once this war is over...

338
00:22:43,711 --> 00:22:46,301
let's cook poultry together.

339
00:22:47,471 --> 00:22:48,631
I'm sure

340
00:22:50,051 --> 00:22:53,431
the king would be...

341
00:22:55,771 --> 00:22:56,851
happy.
